Claude Opus 4.8 in May, Opus 5 in July, Opus 5.5 in September. So I looked into whether the name of the AI model actually changes your answer. What I found is that how hard it thinks matters more. The name is like the badge on a car, and the thinking setting is the gear. On a set of hard tests from Artificial Analysis, the older Claude Opus 5 at its hardest beat the newest Opus 5.5 at its lowest. So why do the apps usually start you low? My guess is that thinking costs OpenAI and Anthropic money, but you pay the same every month. My bet: by the end of June 2027, ChatGPT's free plan goes back to letting the AI pick how hard it thinks for each message. I'm 85% sure.

The full article is free at makeyourmindup.ai. Link in bio.
